;; FH-BEHAVIOR-009 — `:connect` ESTABLISHES the private memory, and nothing
;; else writes it.
;;
;; This is the row the void-returning host mutator needs, and it is the shape
;; almost every library a behavior exists for actually has. `map.setOptions(…)`,
;; `workbook.setValue(…)`, `chart.update(spec)` and `addEventListener` all mutate
;; and answer NOTHING. So a lifecycle entry whose return REPLACED the memory
;; would have the first such call erase the very instance `:disconnect` has to
;; release — silently, in the most ordinary integration there is, and with the
;; leak surfacing only as a host resource nobody released.
;;
;; The probe below is that shape written out honestly (`bv/mutator`): `:connect`
;; builds the instance, and both `:update` and the `:mutate` command are void
;; mutators. The claim is read at `:disconnect`, which is the only moment that
;; matters — the teardown must be handed the instance `:connect` built, after
;; both void returns have come and gone.
;;
;; The mutations are recorded into the instance's OWN mutable cell rather than
;; counted outside it, so `[:update :mutate]` is evidence that the entries ran
;; against the SAME instance rather than merely that some map survived. That
;; cell is also the sanctioned shape for host state that genuinely evolves: the
;; memory is established once, and an adapter that needs it to move returns
;; something mutable.
{:fh/id "FH-BEHAVIOR-009"
 :fh/law
 "A behavior's private memory is ESTABLISHED by `:connect` and nothing else writes it: `:update`, a delivered command and `:disconnect` RECEIVE it and their return values are ignored, so a void-returning host mutator cannot erase the instance the teardown must release."

 ;; The lifecycle transcript (`{:op …}` in order) across the whole probe:
 ;; mount, move the config, deliver one command, unmount.
 :lifecycle [:connect :update :mutate :disconnect]

 ;; What `:connect` established, and what EVERY later entry must still see —
 ;; asserted at each of the three, because a memory that survived the update
 ;; and died at the command would be the same defect one step later.
 :instance :live

 ;; Read off the instance's own cell at `:disconnect`: both void mutators ran,
 ;; in order, against the instance `:connect` built.
 :mutations [:update :mutate]

 ;; The command the probe delivers, and the config move that triggers `:update`.
 :command {:target :probe/mutator :op :mutate}
 :moved   {:label "moved"}}
